[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003074","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003074","source_record_id":"rec_003074","source_corpus":"include","title":"Foundation model for single-cell omics","abstract":"Single-cell sequencing has enabled unprecedented characterization of distinct cell types and advanced our understanding of disease pathogenesis. The rapid expansion of single-cell data presents both tremendous opportunities and challenges. These rich datasets can reveal cellular heterogeneity, enable lineage tracking, and elucidate pathogenic mechanisms for developing personalized therapeutics. However, effectively analyzing the increasingly massive data also requires scalable computational approaches. As single-cell datasets grow exponentially, a critical need emerges to develop methodologies that can harness, enhance, and adapt to these burgeoning resources, and learning the representation for single-cell omics is one of the core tasks for understanding the exponentially growing sequencing data.\n\nThe thesis addresses this need by demonstrating a framework to extract meaningful patterns from the single-cell sequencing data. We develop versatile computational models capable of learning from large-scale data. The key innovation is a pretraining and transfer learning paradigm to construct foundation models tailored to single-cell analysis. We design self-supervised learning tasks to enable scalable training and pre-train the transformer foundation model, scGPT, on diverse unlabeled single-cell data to gain general capabilities. [truncated]","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nSingle-cell sequencing has enabled unprecedented characterization of distinct cell types and advanced our understanding of disease pathogenesis. The rapid expansion of single-cell data presents both tremendous opportunities and challenges. These rich datasets can reveal cellular heterogeneity, enable lineage tracking, and elucidate pathogenic mechanisms for developing personalized therapeutics. However, effectively analyzing the increasingly massive data also requires scalable computational approaches. As single-cell datasets grow exponentially, a critical need emerges to develop methodologies that can harness, enhance, and adapt to these burgeoning resources, and learning the representation for single-cell omics is one of the core tasks for understanding the exponentially growing sequencing data.\n\nThe thesis addresses this need by demonstrating a framework to extract meaningful patterns from the single-cell sequencing data. We develop versatile computational models capable of learning from large-scale data. The key innovation is a pretraining and transfer learning paradigm to construct foundation models tailored to single-cell analysis. We design self-supervised learning tasks to enable scalable training and pre-train the transformer foundation model, scGPT, on diverse unlabeled single-cell data to gain general capabilities. [truncated]\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nThe advent of single-cell omics technologies has enabled biologists to analyze individual cells at an unprecedented resolution. Whereas previous techniques at bulk level could only measure average properties of cell populations, single-cell methods can reveal the tremendous heterogeneity that exists even among seemingly identical cells [Haniffa et al., 2021]. This paradigm shift has also unleashed a torrent of complex genomic datasets capturing biological systems at unprecedented resolution. Effectively analyzing these massive heterogeneous data represents a pressing computational challenge. While deep learning provides a powerful paradigm, prevailing methods rely on simple architectures and objectives ill-suited to single-cell genomics intricacies. This thesis develops a versatile modeling framework to extract meaningful patterns from the explosion of single-cell sequencing data.\n\nThe theme of the thesis recognizes the great opportunity of building large-scale computation models that can match the accumulating of available sequencing data and capture the rules and knowledge of biology in a data-driven manner. For this purpose, we construct the pretraining and transfer learning paradigm for building the foundation model for singlecell data. We adapt the foundation model for various applications across single-cell data analysis and demonstrate both the strong performance of the method and the effectiveness of transfer learning. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\n#### 4.5.1 Analyzing the transfer learning of pre-trained knowledge: the scaling and in-context effects\n\nIn previous sections, scGPT has demonstrated great potential via fine-tuning in a transfer learning manner. We further confirmed the benefits of using the foundation model by comparing it with similar transformer models trained for each downstream task from scratch without pre-training (denoted as scGPT (from-scratch)). The results are presented in Tables 4.3, 4.1, and 4.2, where the fine-tuned scGPT consistently showcases performance gain for tasks such as integration and cell type annotation. Given the observed contributions of the foundation model for downstream tasks, we are further interested in exploring the factors influencing the transfer learning process.\n\nFirstly, we delve into the relationship between pre-training data size and the performance of fine-tuned models: For a certain analysis task, how much improvement can be gained by further adding sequencing data into atlases for pre-training. We pre-trained a series of scGPT models of the same number of parameters but using different sizes of data, from 30K to 33M sequenced normal human cells. Figure 4.7A illustrates the resulting performance of fine-tuning on various applications using these different pre-trained models. We observed the performance of fine-tuned models improved as the size of the pre-training data increased (Box 4.1). [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nIn previous chapters, we demonstrated the benefits of pre-training on single-cell data with comprehensive experiments in both zero-shot and fine-tuning settings. The pre-trained model itself is a universal feature extractor. It showcases strong capabilities of extrapolating to unseen datasets, presenting meaningful cell clustering in zero-shot experiments. In addition, the learned gene networks in scGPT exhibit strong alignment with known functional connections. The model's ability to accurately capture gene-gene interactions and reflect known biological knowledge provides confidence in its capacity to uncover meaningful insights in single-cell biology. We also observed a universal contribution of the pretrained knowledge onto multiple downstream tasks in a transfer learning manner.\n\nFurthermore, the pre-trained model's knowledge can be transferred to multiple downstream tasks through fine-tuning. In tasks such as cell type annotation, multi-batch, and multi-omic integration, the fine-tuned scGPT model consistently outperforms models trained from scratch. This demonstrates the valuable contribution of the pre-trained model to downstream tasks, enabling more accurate and biologically meaningful analyses. Overall, our experiments highlight the efficacy of pre-training in scGPT, showcasing its ability to generalize, capture gene networks, and improve performance in downstream tasks through transfer learning. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Data collection]\nWe collected data for the whole-human foundation model pre-training from the CELLxGENE portal [Chanzuckerberg Initiative., 2022] using the Census API 1 . We included sequencing protocols of scRNA-seq and snRNA-seq and filtered in samples without disease conditions. This resulted in sequencing data of 33 million cells. We also trained several organ-specific models on samples from individual organs and tissues. For example, we retrieved over 10.3 million human blood and bone marrow scRNA-seq samples [Chanzuckerberg Initiative., 2022] for the pre-training of the scGPT blood model specifically. A total of 65 datasets were collected from CELLxGENE by filtering on Organism (i.e., Homo sapiens), Tissue (i.e., blood, bone marrow), and Disease (i.e., normal, COVID-19, influenza). Additionally, we collected 5.7 million cells of various cancer types to train a pan-cancer model.\n\n[data_representation: Box 4.1: Influence of pre-training data size]\nTo investigate the effect of pre-training data size on scGPT's performance, we pretrained the scGPT model on smaller scales of the normal human cells, i.e. 30K, 300K and 3M cells. The resultant models were evaluated in both zero-shot and fine-tuned configurations across multiple tasks. Figure 4.7 reveals that, with growth in pre-training data size, scGPT has increasing performance for both zero-shot and fine-tuning scenarios.\n\nWe demonstrate the influence of pre-training data size spanning various tasks of cell type annotation, reference mapping, and perturbation response prediction. Constant trends of increasing fine-tuning performance were observed across all datasets (Figure 4.7A-C). On cell type annotation (utilizing softmax classifiers) and reference mapping, we observed significant increases regarding all evaluation metrics. On the other hand, the performance gain on perturbation is marginal. From the 30k data pre-training to 33M, the result fine-tuning differences are less than 7%, exemplified by the Pearson delta improvement from 0.229 to 0.242. One of the reasons can be the Perturb-seq data sparsity, which we further depicted in Section 4.5.3.\n\nNext, we examined the performance of zero-shot settings for reference mapping and integration. For these two tasks, the cell embeddings computed from the pre-trained scGPT can be used to compute either the nearest neighbors for propagating reference labels, or for integrating similar cells across samples. We visualize the performances when using different pre-trained models in Figure 4.7D,E. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Summary of benchmarking datasets]\nWe provide the list of datasets used in the benchmarking experiments in this chapter.\n\nPBMC 10K The PBMC 10K dataset comprises two scRNA-seq batches of human peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) obtained from a healthy donor. The dataset was re-processed by Gayoso et al. [Gayoso et al., 2022], resulting in 3,346 differentially expressed genes. The first batch contains 7,982 cells, while the second batch contains 4,008 cells. The cell groups annotated using Seurat [Satija et al., 2015] consist of 9 categories, namely B cells, CD4 T cells, CD8 T cells, CD14+ Monocytes, Dendritic Cells, NK cells, FCGR3A+ Monocytes, Megakaryocytes, and Other.\n\nPerirhinal Cortex The Perirhinal Cortex dataset includes two distinct samples, drawn from a larger study by Siletti et al. [Siletti et al., 2022], which originally contained 606 highquality samples encompassing ten diverse brain regions. Each of the two selected batches from the Perirhinal Cortex dataset comprises a substantial number of cells, with the first batch consisting of 8,465 cells and the second batch containing 9,070 cells. An extensive range of 59,357 genes are incorporated within these datasets. We have made use of the annotations of ten unique cell types provided in the original study.\n\nCOVID-19 The COVID-19 dataset, derived from work by Lotfollahi et al. [Lotfollahi et al., 2022], is organized into 18 distinct batches and offers a diverse representation of cells from lung tissue, PBMC, and bone marrow. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Model Architecture and Training]\nRetaining the foundation model's architecture, our continual pretraining maintains an embedding size of 512, constituted by 12 stacked transformer blocks, each equipped with 8 attention heads. The fully connected layer, pivotal in processing the embeddings, also maintains a hidden size of 512. We adopt a data partitioning strategy where 99% of the spatial omics data is allocated for training, with the remaining 1% reserved for validation purposes. This ensures a robust training regime while allowing for periodic assessment of the model's generalization capabilities on unseen data.\n\nA critical aspect of our training approach involves handling input gene length. The maximum input length during training is set to 1200 genes, we employ a random sampling technique for cells exhibiting a gene count exceeding this threshold. This randomization is performed iteratively, ensuring diverse gene representation across training epochs. Furthermore, the ratio of genes to be generated is varied, sampled uniformly from three options: 0.25, 0.50, and 0.75. This variability in gene sampling ratios introduces a level of stochasticity, promoting model robustness and preventing overfitting to specific gene expression patterns.\n\nThe optimization of the model is conducted using the Adam optimizer, a decision underscored by its effectiveness in handling sparse gradients and adapting learning rates. The training is conducted with a mini-batch size of 32, striking a balance between computational efficiency and gradient stability. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":4,"line_start":13,"line_end":20,"text":"Single-cell sequencing has enabled unprecedented characterization of distinct cell types and advanced our understanding of disease pathogenesis. The rapid expansion of single-cell data presents both tremendous opportunities and challenges. These rich datasets can reveal cellular heterogeneity, enable lineage tracking, and elucidate pathogenic mechanisms for developing personalized therapeutics. However, effectively analyzing the increasingly massive data also requires scalable computational approaches. As single-cell datasets grow exponentially, a critical need emerges to develop methodologies that can harness, enhance, and adapt to these burgeoning resources, and learning the representation for single-cell omics is one of the core tasks for understanding the exponentially growing sequencing data.\n\nThe thesis addresses this need by demonstrating a framework to extract meaningful patterns from the single-cell sequencing data. We develop versatile computational models capable of learning from large-scale data. The key innovation is a pretraining and transfer learning paradigm to construct foundation models tailored to single-cell analysis. We design self-supervised learning tasks to enable scalable training and pre-train the transformer foundation model, scGPT, on diverse unlabeled single-cell data to gain general capabilities. [truncated]","original_chars":2056},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"Introduction","heading_level":2,"line_start":237,"line_end":254,"text":"The advent of single-cell omics technologies has enabled biologists to analyze individual cells at an unprecedented resolution. Whereas previous techniques at bulk level could only measure average properties of cell populations, single-cell methods can reveal the tremendous heterogeneity that exists even among seemingly identical cells [Haniffa et al., 2021]. This paradigm shift has also unleashed a torrent of complex genomic datasets capturing biological systems at unprecedented resolution. Effectively analyzing these massive heterogeneous data represents a pressing computational challenge. While deep learning provides a powerful paradigm, prevailing methods rely on simple architectures and objectives ill-suited to single-cell genomics intricacies. This thesis develops a versatile modeling framework to extract meaningful patterns from the explosion of single-cell sequencing data.\n\nThe theme of the thesis recognizes the great opportunity of building large-scale computation models that can match the accumulating of available sequencing data and capture the rules and knowledge of biology in a data-driven manner. For this purpose, we con